WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.650
Picture a toy machine with five rules and two symbols.

2
00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:14.500
One of them runs for exactly forty-seven million one hundred seventy-six thousand eight hundred seventy steps and then stops — and working out which one stops took humanity sixty years,

3
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:18.630
and in the end a proof we had to hand to a computer to verify.

4
00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:25.640
As for the next number, nobody knows it anymore; because that is where mathematics runs into a wall of its own making.

5
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:44.370
Kanal ayarı gereği bir not: `podcast-science.json` içinde bu kanal `"language": "en"` olarak tanımlı ve yönergesi "aynı günün bilim bölümü çevrilir" diyor.

6
00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:57.140
Kaynak metin (`bilim.txt`) zaten Türkçe olduğu için "Türkçe'ye çeviri" boş bir işlem olurdu; bu yüzden metni kanalın gerçek dili olan İngilizce'ye, kaynağa sadık kalarak çevirdim.

7
00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:04.310
Açılış kancası, jingle ve kapanış cümlesi sizin eklediğiniz parçalar olduğu için çıkarıldı.

8
00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:13.400
In 1962, a Hungarian-born mathematician teaching at Ohio State University, Tibor Radó, proposed a game to his students.

9
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:20.090
The rules were as simple as a child's game, and the consequences deep enough to press against the limits of mathematics.

10
00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:24.060
Imagine you have a blank tape stretching out forever.

11
00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:30.110
The tape is divided into individual cells, and sitting on one of those cells is a read-write head.

12
00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:37.440
The head looks at the cell beneath it, sees whether there is a 0 or a 1 there, and then consults the table of rules it has been given.

13
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:46.210
The table tells it three things: what to write in that cell, whether to move right or left, and which mood, which state, to move into on the next step.

14
00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:48.260
That is all.

15
00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:53.990
We call this a Turing machine, and in theory it can do everything every computer in the world can do.

16
00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:56.680
Radó's question was this.

17
00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:03.210
Suppose the machine has only 5 different states, meaning the rule table has only 5 rows.

18
00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:06.700
There are a limited number of ways to fill in that table.

19
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:12.110
Some tables send the machine into an endless loop, and it shuttles back and forth forever.

20
00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:15.600
Others bring it to a halt at some point.

21
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.570
Focus on the ones that halt, Radó said.

22
00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:23.220
Among those, which one runs the longest?

23
00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.750
How many steps does it take?

24
00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:29.400
How many 1s does it write on the paper before it shuts down?

25
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.210
He called this number the busy beaver number.

26
00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:41.980
Because the machine, out on that infinite blank tape, keeps writing and erasing, going back and forth, just as a beaver hauls branches to a streambed.

27
00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:49.470
The paper Radó published in 1962 on non-computable functions revealed a bomb hidden inside this seemingly harmless game.

28
00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:52.160
The bomb was this.

29
00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:58.690
If you knew the maximum number of steps a 5-state machine could take, you would hold enormous power in your hands.

30
00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:05.220
Hand someone a 5-state machine you had never seen before, and you would run it and wait exactly that many steps.

31
00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.430
If it halts, it halts.

32
00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:12.360
If it has not halted by that number, you would know with certainty that it never will.

33
00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:17.130
Because by definition that number is the longest any halting machine runs.

34
00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:25.100
So without ever examining the program, just by watching the clock, you could say whether any given program would fall into an infinite loop.

35
00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:30.670
And yet Alan Turing had proved in 1936 that precisely this is impossible.

36
00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:36.400
There is no general method that tells you in every case whether a program halts, and there cannot be one.

37
00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:43.250
Which means there can be no formula, no algorithm, no computer program that hands you all the busy beaver numbers.

38
00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:45.620
The function is uncomputable.

39
00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:50.230
And not merely uncomputable: it grows at a rate beyond comprehension.

40
00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:54.840
Every computable function you can think of, it eventually leaves behind.

41
00:03:54.840 --> 00:04:00.890
But here is the crucial point: being uncomputable does not mean none of its values can be known.

42
00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:06.460
One by one, by hand, by sheer labor, you can pin down some of them.

43
00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:10.590
And mathematicians set out to do exactly that.

44
00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:13.600
The first steps were easy.

45
00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:16.930
The record for a 1-state machine is 1 step.

46
00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:20.260
For a 2-state machine, 6 steps.

47
00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:23.430
The third step began to fight back.

48
00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:29.960
Radó's doctoral student Shen Lin combed through every 3-state machine and proved that the record was 21 steps.

49
00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:35.210
They published the result together in 1965, shortly before Radó's death.

50
00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:38.380
The fourth step took 18 years.

51
00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:44.910
In 1983 Allen Brady showed that the record for 4-state machines was 107 steps.

52
00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:49.520
1, 6, 21, 107.

53
00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:55.890
At first glance, a sequence that looks harmless enough, not growing with any particular cruelty.

54
00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:58.100
And then came the wall.

55
00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:06.230
In 1989 two German researchers, Heiner Marxen and Jürgen Buntrock, found the monster among the 5-state machines.

56
00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:19.960
Its rule table still had just 5 rows, it still wrote nothing but 0s and 1s, but this machine ran on a blank tape for exactly 47,176,870 steps and then stopped without warning.

57
00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:24.410
When it halted, 4098 ones were left on the tape.

58
00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:27.580
From 107 to 47 million.

59
00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:34.110
Adding a single row, a single state, had multiplied the scale of the game half a million times over.

60
00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.960
Everyone believed this was the record.

61
00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:40.930
Searches went on for years and nothing longer turned up.

62
00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:44.420
But believing is one thing and proving is another.

63
00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:51.590
To say that this number truly is the record, you have to show that not one of the 5-state machines runs longer than that and then halts.

64
00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:57.000
Which means proving, for every single machine, that it either halts or runs forever.

65
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.010
And that was where the difficulty lay.

66
00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:05.580
Showing that a machine halts is easy: you run it, it stops, done.

67
00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:10.990
But showing that a machine will never halt is making a claim about an infinite future.

68
00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:14.640
However long you run it, you cannot know the answer.

69
00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:20.850
You have to understand it, unravel the pattern inside it, capture its loop mathematically.

70
00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:24.340
And you had to do this millions of times over.

71
00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:28.310
The total number of 5-state machines runs into the billions.

72
00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:36.600
Once you strip away symmetries, duplicates, and tables identical to one another, roughly 120 million candidates remain.

73
00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:41.370
About 88 million of these had to be decided one by one.

74
00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:45.820
At one point Allen Brady feared the number might never be known at all.

75
00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:54.270
Because if even one of those millions of machines had behavior tied to an unsolved mathematical problem like the Goldbach conjecture, the door would close.

76
00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:57.280
For 40 years nobody opened that door.

77
00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:00.930
Then a crowd from outside academia opened it.

78
00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:10.660
In the spring of 2022, Tristan Stérin, who had just finished his doctoral work, set up an internet forum and a chat server devoted to the busy beaver problem.

79
00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:13.830
The effort was called the Busy Beaver Challenge.

80
00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:19.720
The aim was clear: to clear out the 5-state machines en masse, through distributed effort.

81
00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:23.210
Most of the people who showed up had no academic title.

82
00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:28.780
Software developers, former mathematics students, curious amateurs.

83
00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:32.110
Shawn Ligocki was a software engineer.

84
00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:36.720
Justin Blanchard had an unfinished graduate background in mathematics.

85
00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:41.810
Maja Kądziołka was a self-taught 21-year-old Polish programmer.

86
00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:45.300
Their numbers passed 20.

87
00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:48.150
The method was this.

88
00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:53.720
Instead of solving machines one at a time, they wrote programs they called deciders.

89
00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:58.010
Each decider recognized a particular pattern of behavior.

90
00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:04.220
One program to catch a certain shape repeating on the tape forever, another to catch a different kind of loop.

91
00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:09.310
Every new decider wiped millions of machines off the remaining pile in a single stroke.

92
00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:17.280
The pile fell from millions to hundreds of thousands, from hundreds of thousands to thousands, from thousands to a few dozen.

93
00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:20.770
The last survivors became legendary.

94
00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:31.140
Two machines from a list of hard cases compiled years earlier by a Bulgarian researcher under the pseudonym Skelet, Skelet 1 and Skelet 17, resisted every attack.

95
00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:35.750
Skelet 17 was cracked by Chris Xu, a graduate student.

96
00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:43.400
But the real turning point came from a participant nobody could identify, who appeared only under the handle mxdys.

97
00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:54.410
mxdys raised the community's standard: every claim had to be not an argument a human could read and be persuaded by, but a formal proof a machine could check line by line.

98
00:08:54.410 --> 00:09:00.780
In May 2024, mxdys completed a colossal proof, 40,000 lines long.

99
00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:09.390
It was written in the proof checker called Coq, a system in which every logical step is independently tested by the computer and nothing is left to human intuition.

100
00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:17.040
On an ordinary laptop, using 13 cores, that checker verified the entire proof in about 45 minutes.

101
00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:21.810
On July 2, 2024, the result was announced.

102
00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:27.860
The fifth busy beaver number is 47,176,870.

103
00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:33.590
The machine Marxen and Buntrock had found 35 years earlier really was the champion.

104
00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:37.720
62 years had passed since Radó asked the question.

105
00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:47.770
The winner was not the elegant discovery of a single genius, but the collective bookkeeping of a community, spread over years, most of whose names were nothing but pseudonyms.

106
00:09:47.770 --> 00:09:54.460
The victory had not even been announced yet when it became clear that something far darker was waiting on the next step of the hunt.

107
00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:57.470
Nobody knows how large the sixth step is.

108
00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:02.880
The only thing known is that the estimates coming from below have long since abandoned human intuition.

109
00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:13.730
In 2022 Pavel Kropitz showed that the number of 1s a 6-state machine writes on the tape is larger than a tower of 10s stacked 15 high, a 10 raised to a 10 raised to a 10 and so on.

110
00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:18.180
Next to that number, the count of atoms in the universe is a rounding error.

111
00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:28.870
In 2025 mxdys broke the bound once again and pushed it to a level that cannot be expressed even with towers of exponents, a level that requires counting towers of towers.

112
00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:31.720
But the real issue is not size.

113
00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:39.210
The real issue surfaced on June 28, 2024, just four days before the fifth number was officially announced.

114
00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:45.260
On the community's chat server, mxdys mentioned a strange 6-state machine.

115
00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:50.990
The machine neither halted nor fell into any recognizable loop; it behaved almost at random.

116
00:10:50.990 --> 00:10:55.920
A participant named Racheline soon worked out the machine's hidden rule.

117
00:10:55.920 --> 00:11:01.330
They named it Antihydra, because it closely resembled an earlier machine called Hydra.

118
00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:05.300
What Antihydra does can be reduced to a plain arithmetic game.

119
00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:08.310
You have a number, 8 to begin with.

120
00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:15.320
Each round you take that number, compute its half, throw away the remainder, and add what you get back to the number itself.

121
00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:19.450
So the number grows by roughly one and a half times each round.

122
00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:22.780
On the side you also keep a counter.

123
00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:29.950
If the number in your hand that round is even, you increase the counter by 2; if it is odd, you decrease it by 1.

124
00:11:29.950 --> 00:11:34.560
The machine halts if and only if that counter drops below zero.

125
00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:43.650
So for Antihydra to halt, the odd numbers in this sequence growing by a factor of one and a half have to leave the even ones behind by an overwhelming margin.

126
00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:49.220
It is a race in which the counter gains 2 for every even and loses 1 for every odd.

127
00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:55.750
If odds and evens arrive with roughly equal frequency, the counter climbs on average, steadily.

128
00:11:55.750 --> 00:12:00.360
For it to fall, a long run of odd numbers has to come up back to back.

129
00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:03.690
Probability gives a very clear answer here.

130
00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:14.700
If odd and even really are distributed like a coin toss, the chance that the counter ever dips below zero is one in the number you get by writing 10 followed by 200 million zeros.

131
00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:17.390
In practice, zero.

132
00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:21.200
The machine almost certainly runs forever.

133
00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:24.850
Except that almost certainly means nothing in mathematics.

134
00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:28.500
These numbers are not being produced by flipping coins.

135
00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:33.430
It is a fully determined sequence, born from a single starting value.

136
00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:40.440
Someone has to prove that the distribution of odds and evens really does behave as though it were random, and nobody can.

137
00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:48.570
What is more, this question connects directly to a problem that has stood open in mathematics for decades, Mahler's question about Z numbers,

138
00:12:48.570 --> 00:12:53.180
about how the fractional parts of the powers of one and a half are distributed.

139
00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:57.470
Shawn Ligocki gave machines like this a name: cryptids.

140
00:12:57.470 --> 00:13:05.120
Like creatures said to exist in the forest but never captured, they are machines whose behavior you can observe but cannot prove.

141
00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:08.130
Antihydra is the most famous of them.

142
00:13:08.130 --> 00:13:11.620
And on its own it locks the door to the sixth step.

143
00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:17.350
If you want to know the sixth busy beaver number, you first have to solve this arithmetic puzzle.

144
00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:20.840
The beaver hunt has turned into a mathematics problem.

145
00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:24.810
As the scale climbs, the picture grows heavier still.

146
00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:35.180
Through Stefan O'Rear's work, it was shown that a 27-state Turing machine searches for a counterexample to the Goldbach conjecture, meaning it halts only if that conjecture is false.

147
00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:40.750
With about 744 states the same thing can be done for the Riemann hypothesis.

148
00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:52.400
And the most striking of all: there is a 745-state machine for which the question of whether it halts can neither be proved nor disproved from the set theory axioms on which modern mathematics is built.

149
00:13:52.400 --> 00:14:00.370
O'Rear established this result, and Johannes Riebel wrote it up in detail in his undergraduate thesis and brought it down one more step.

150
00:14:00.370 --> 00:14:02.420
Here is what that means.

151
00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:09.910
The 745th busy beaver number is, by definition, a specific, finite, single whole number.

152
00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:13.560
It stops somewhere, it has a value.

153
00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:19.290
But from inside the mathematics we use today, we will never be able to learn what that value is.

154
00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:22.140
A number proved to be unprovable.

155
00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:25.950
And this is where the real worth of the busy beaver function comes out.

156
00:14:25.950 --> 00:14:31.200
It is not an amusing puzzle but a ruler that measures mathematics' own difficulty.

157
00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:35.810
Every open conjecture corresponds to some rung on that ruler.

158
00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:44.740
Goldbach at 27, Riemann at 744, the boundary of what mathematics can know at 745.

159
00:14:44.740 --> 00:14:48.230
A hunt lasting 60 years won the fifth step.

160
00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:58.920
On the sixth the hunt is not over; a handful of anonymous people from every corner of the world are learning together at exactly what point the hunt stops being a hunt and becomes the boundary itself.

161
00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:02.890
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

162
00:15:02.890 --> 00:15:06.540
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
